{# A refresh of the list gets the list, a navigation of the list frame gets the
   document with only the list in its body, and an ordinary visit gets the whole
   page. All three render the same `list_frame` block below, so the answers
   cannot drift apart — see App\Twig\ListFragmentGlobal for what each caller
   reads, and for why the middle one keeps its head. #}
{% extends listFragment.poll ? '_layout/_list_fragment.html.twig' : '_layout/app.html.twig' %}

{# The list/reading pane switch belongs to these pages alone: its targets are
   further down this file, and it throws on connect wherever they are not.
   Spelled out rather than `parent() ~ mail--mail-pane`, because the parent is
   conditional above and the fragment layout has no such block to inherit. #}
{#
A mail list is never restored from Turbo's snapshot cache.

Opening a conversation is an ordinary Drive visit, so Turbo snapshots the list
as it leaves — and it leaves BEFORE the conversation is marked read, because
mail--thread-read waits two seconds and then posts. The stream that post renders
back targets a row that is not on the thread page, so nothing updates the
snapshot either. Pressing the browser's back button then restored it: the row
still bold, the unread count still counting it, and no request made that could
have corrected either.

A poll does correct it, a few seconds later, which is what made this "sometimes"
— and what makes it easy to write a test that passes against the bug. The list
is stale at the moment it is shown, and that is the whole of the complaint.

`no-cache` means back re-fetches. That costs one request on a screen whose only
purpose is to show current state, and whose contents may have changed for a
dozen other reasons while the conversation was open.
#}
{% block turbo_cache_control %}
    <meta name="turbo-cache-control" content="no-cache">
{% endblock %}

{# The base layout puts the Inbox's unread count in front of every title, which
   is right for settings or the calendar and wrong here: a mail folder says its
   own number or none (the bin counts a total, Sent and Archive count nothing),
   and the Inbox says its own in its own title block. Off by default for all of
   them, and each page that does count names its key again, as before. #}
{% block title_prefix %}{% endblock %}
{% block title_count_key %}{% endblock %}

{# mail--dnd is here rather than in the base layout for the same reason
   mail--mail-pane is: it is about a mail LIST. It listens on <body> because
   the two ends of a drag — the rows in the list frame and the folders in the
   sidebar, of which there are two copies — have no closer common ancestor. #}
{# `parent()`, and not the base list written out again. Written out, it was
   one controller short: the base layout mounts `mail--jobs` beside
   `core--mercure`, this block replaced both names with its own three, and the
   topbar's background-work indicator was never told to look again on any mail
   page — which is every page a bulk action is started from. It showed what the
   server rendered at page load and then sat there, for as long as it existed
   (#42 is where it was finally noticed). Whatever the base layout mounts, the
   mailbox mounts too. #}
{# mail--sender-pictures here and not in the base layout: the avatars it
   updates exist only on mail pages (#639). #}
{% block body_controllers %}{{ parent() }} mail--mail-pane mail--dnd mail--sender-pictures{% endblock %}

{% block body_values %}
    {# The list to draw beside a message that was opened by its own address.
       A thread page has no list behind it — the route knows a conversation, not
       the folder and page it was opened from — so with the reading pane beside
       the list, a reload or a bookmark would otherwise leave an empty column
       next to the message. The Inbox is the one list that always exists. #}
    data-mail--mail-pane-inbox-url-value="{{ path('app_mail_inbox') }}"
    {# Two whole URLs rather than a base the controller appends an action to.
       A route is the router's to spell, and `path()` cannot be asked for a
       prefix — building one by trimming the end off a generated path is a
       string operation that keeps working right up until the route changes. #}
    data-mail--dnd-move-url-value="{{ path('app_status_bulk_run', { action: 'move' }) }}"
    data-mail--dnd-label-url-value="{{ path('app_status_bulk_run', { action: 'label' }) }}"
    data-mail--dnd-category-url-value="{{ path('app_status_bulk_run', { action: 'category' }) }}"
    data-mail--dnd-read-url-value="{{ path('app_status_bulk_run', { action: 'read' }) }}"
    data-mail--dnd-star-url-value="{{ path('app_status_bulk_run', { action: 'star' }) }}"
    {# The placeholder is left in deliberately — the controller substitutes it.
       Same shape as the calendar grid's pending/reverted announcements. Always
       plural because the pill it fills is only drawn for two or more; a single
       conversation drags a picture of its own row. #}
    {# Emails, not conversations, in the email list (#615). #}
    data-mail--dnd-dragging-value="{{ (app.user and not app.user.conversationView ? 'dnd.dragging_emails' : 'dnd.dragging')|trans }}"
{% endblock %}

{# The one part of a view that varies within its scope: the category tab for the
   inbox, the label id for a label view. Empty for every list where the scope is
   the whole answer. Defined here so `block('list_scope_value')` resolves for
   every list rather than only the two that care. #}
{% block list_scope_value %}{% endblock %}

{# What the toolbar's "select all N" posts as the view, which is list_scope and
   list_scope_value everywhere except a search. There list_scope has to stay
   `*` — it is also the sync scope, and a search shows mail from every mailbox
   — while the toolbar has to name the search and its query, or the worker is
   handed `*` and resolves it to no conversations at all (#711). Two blocks of
   their own rather than a second meaning for the two above: those reach the
   frame too, where every row button reads them (assets/list_view.js). #}
{% block list_view_scope %}{{ block('list_scope') }}{% endblock %}
{% block list_view_value %}{{ block('list_scope_value') }}{% endblock %}

{% set selectedMessage = selectedMessage|default(null) %}

{#
Subclasses can override these blocks:
  inbox_tabs      — tabs / heading row above the toolbar (renders before toolbar)
  inbox_toolbar   — override the entire toolbar (rarely needed)
  message_list    — the <li> items inside the <ul>
  list_scope      — space-separated special-use roles this view shows, so a
                    Mercure mailbox.synced update knows whether to refresh
                    ("inbox", "sent", … or "*" for every mailbox; default "*")
  reading_pane    — the reading pane content
  pane_open       — "true"/"false" string (default false)
  list_hidden     — extra CSS classes to hide list on direct-load pages
  reading_hidden  — extra CSS classes (default: "hidden")

Pagination variables expected from the controller (all optional, fallback to 0/1):
  page, total, per_page
#}

{#
Which of the two documents this is.

A frame navigation of the list — a folder in the sidebar, a category tab, the
pager, the sort menu, a search — has Turbo take `inbox-list-frame` out of the
response and drop everything else in the body on the floor. Rendering the
sidebar, the topbar and the calendar pane for it is work whose output is
measured, parsed and discarded — 21–24 database statements on a fifty-row
list against the 9–13 the frame alone costs, spent on a navigation that cannot
have changed any of it.

The HEAD is not dropped, which is why this is a fork inside `body` rather than
a second parent layout. Turbo reads that head to decide whether the navigation
renders anything at all, and then merges it into the live document, removing
what the response leaves out — so it has to stay exactly what app.html.twig
renders for a full visit. Cut it down to the <title> and the tab stops
following the folder you clicked; cut it down further and the page loses its
csrf token. Both measured: see App\Twig\ListFragmentGlobal.
#}
{% block body %}{% if listFragment.navigation %}{{ block('list_frame') }}{% else %}{{ block('mailbox_page') }}{% endif %}{% endblock %}

{% block mailbox_page %}
    {#
    sidebar-drawer wraps the entire page so the controller can manage
    the drawer state from any trigger (burger button in the topbar, backdrop).
    #}
    <div
        class="h-full overflow-hidden app-bg"
        data-controller="ui--sidebar-drawer"
    >
        {# ── Mobile backdrop ──────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
        <div
            data-ui--sidebar-drawer-target="backdrop"
            data-action="click->ui--sidebar-drawer#backdropClick"
            class="md:hidden fixed inset-0 z-30 bg-black/40 backdrop-blur-sm
                   opacity-0 pointer-events-none transition-opacity duration-200"
            aria-hidden="true"
        ></div>

        {# ── Mobile sidebar drawer ────────────────────────────────────────── #}
        {#
        On mobile: fixed, full-height, slides in from the left.
        On md+: hidden (the normal sidebar is in the flex row below).
        #}
        {# `data-surface` — see app.css. Each of the three marks a wrapper that
           re-points the density tokens at that surface's own resolved pair, so
           a dense list can sit beside a comfortable reading pane. On the
           wrappers rather than inside the partials because the cascade reaches
           the same elements either way and _sidebar.html.twig is a file this
           change deliberately leaves alone. Both sidebar copies carry it: the
           drawer is the same sidebar on a phone, and a setting that applied to
           one and not the other would be a bug nobody could describe. #}
        <div
            data-ui--sidebar-drawer-target="drawer" id="sidebar-drawer" inert
            data-surface="sidebar"
            class="md:hidden fixed inset-y-0 left-0 z-40 w-64 p-3
                   -translate-x-full transition-transform duration-200 ease-out"
            aria-label="{{ 'sidebar.nav.inbox'|trans }}"
        >
            {# The workspace's own sidebar, as on the desktop (#783); a workspace
               without one has an empty drawer, and its burger is not drawn. #}
            {% include '_layout/_sidebar_frame.html.twig' with { drawer: true } %}
        </div>

        {#
        ── The workspace (#755) ──────────────────────────────────────────────

        The row beside the sidebar is the user's active workspace: a tree of
        splits (side by side, or above and below) with one pane at each leaf,
        drawn from the layout global. It replaced a fixed row — the mail, then
        the calendar, the address book and the tasks docked to its right, each
        with its own three-way switch in the topbar and its own controller
        negotiating room with the others.

        Every pane is rendered exactly once below, into `bodies`, and placed by
        _workspace.html.twig: the ones in the workspace into the tree, the rest
        into the park, hidden and lazy. layout--workspace moves those same
        sections around when the layout changes — it never re-renders a pane —
        so a split or a move keeps every frame, scroll position and open
        contact as it was.

        The controller is on this element, not the root, because the topbar's
        workspace tabs and Layout button are inside it and a Stimulus action
        only reaches a controller on an ancestor.
        #}
        {% import '_layout/_workspace.html.twig' as workspace %}
        {% set kinds = {} %}
        {% for kind in enum('App\\Domain\\Enum\\Layout\\PaneKind').cases() %}
            {% set kinds = kinds|merge({ (kind.value): kind }) %}
        {% endfor %}
        {% set activeWorkspace = app.user ? layout.active : null %}
        {# The panes on screen by key (LayoutPane::key()): a kind, or
           `calendar~<id>` for each calendar after the first. #}
        {% set present = activeWorkspace ? activeWorkspace.root.keys : ['list'] %}
        {#
        Full width. A page that IS one pane — /contacts, /tasks — names it as
        `fullPane` and is drawn with it at full width whatever the device
        remembers; otherwise it is what this device last put there, if that
        pane is still in the workspace.
        #}
        {% set fullPane = fullPane|default(null) %}
        {% set maxKind = fullPane ?? (app.user and layout.maximized ? layout.maximized : null) %}
        {#
        Whether the open message has a pane of its own beside the list. With
        no Message pane in the workspace — or with the list at full width — a
        conversation opens in place of the list, as it did with the reading
        pane off, and the message's element is drawn inside the list's card.
        layout--workspace moves it between the two when that changes; below lg
        it always lives in the list's card, because there the panes take turns.
        #}
        {% set beside = 'message' in present and maxKind != 'list' %}
        {# Below lg, the pane shown first: the page's own, else the list, else
           whatever comes first that is not the message (the message is never
           a tab — it opens in place of the list there). #}
        {% set tabKinds = present|filter(kind => kind != 'message') %}
        {% set firstTab = fullPane ?? ('list' in present ? 'list' : (tabKinds|first ?: present|first)) %}
        {% set paneOpen = 'true' == block('pane_open')|trim %}

        {% set readingElement %}
            <div
                data-mail--mail-pane-target="reading"
                data-surface="reading"
                class="flex flex-col flex-1 overflow-hidden {% block reading_hidden %}hidden{% endblock %}"
            >
                {% block reading_pane %}
                    {% if selectedMessage is not null %}
                        {% include 'mail/_message_content.html.twig' with { message: selectedMessage } %}
                    {% endif %}
                {% endblock %}
            </div>
        {% endset %}

        {% set bodies = {} %}

        {# ── The mail list ───────────────────────────────────────────────
           The insight strip, then the list's card. Two cards rather than a
           band inside one, so the strip carries its own border and sits on the
           app background the way every pane does. The strip's frame is lazy
           and renders nothing when there is nothing to say; its separation is
           the strip's own margin (insight/_pane.html.twig), so an absent strip
           costs no gap. #}
        {% set listBody %}
            <section class="layout-pane flex flex-col min-w-0 min-h-0" {{ workspace.attrs(kinds.list) }} {{ maxKind == 'list' ? 'data-maxed' }} {{ firstTab == 'list' ? 'data-tab-current' }}>
                {# insight--pane on the frame, not inside it: the frame is the
                   one element every reload of the strip keeps, so the
                   controller is there to hear `insights.changed` even when
                   the strip loaded empty. The tokens cost no query. #}
                <turbo-frame id="insight_pane"
                             class="shrink-0"
                             src="{{ path('app_insight_pane') }}"
                             loading="lazy"
                             {% if app.user %}
                             data-controller="insight--pane"
                             data-insight--pane-token-value="{{ csrf_token('insight-dismiss') }}"
                             data-insight--pane-pane-token-value="{{ csrf_token('insight-pane-dismiss') }}"
                             data-insight--pane-pane-url-value="{{ path('app_insight_pane_dismiss') }}"
                             {% endif %}></turbo-frame>

                {# `data-reading-open` is the one thing that varies within a
                   layout: a message is open. The thread page renders it
                   directly; everywhere else mail--mail-pane sets and clears it
                   on whichever card holds the message, so the placeholder and
                   the message never both show. #}
                <div class="main-pane layout-card flex flex-1 overflow-hidden relative"
                     data-mail-card
                     {% if paneOpen and not beside %}data-reading-open{% endif %}>

                    {# List pane #}
                    <div
                        id="message-list"
                        data-surface="list"
                        data-mail--mail-pane-target="list"
                        {# Confirms to the server that the rows below reached the
                           screen. Mounted on the container rather than on each
                           row so fifty rows are one report, and here rather than
                           inside the frame so it survives a fragment swap and
                           watches for one. #}
                        {# One data-controller attribute: a second one is dropped by the parser. The
                           pull gesture is outside the frame, so a refresh cannot take it away, and
                           it reaches the topbar's sync button by lookup, not by DOM position. #}
                        {# The row swipe is outside the frame for the same reason, and one delegated
                           listener serves every row, so a refresh cannot leave a row without it. #}
                        {# The long-press is outside the frame for the same reason, and it opens the action sheet, which
                           is copied from the template at the end of this frame: a sheet opened by a hold is always the
                           list's current one. #}
                        {% if app.user %}
                            data-controller="mail--new-marker mail--pull-to-refresh mail--row-swipe mail--row-hold"
                            data-mail--new-marker-url-value="{{ path('app_mail_threads_listed') }}"
                        {% else %}
                            data-controller="mail--pull-to-refresh mail--row-swipe mail--row-hold"
                        {% endif %}
                        {# @container, so the rows below switch to their stacked
                           layout on the LIST's width rather than the viewport's:
                           a workspace with three panes side by side leaves the
                           list narrower than a phone on a wide window. #}
                        class="@container relative flex flex-col flex-1 overflow-hidden {% block list_hidden %}{% endblock %}"
                    >
                        {# The pull to refresh indicator. Here, beside the frame and not inside it, so a
                           refresh never replaces it. Its words are the visual states only: the sync
                           toast is what announces the run, so this stays out of the accessibility tree. #}
                        <div data-mail--pull-to-refresh-target="indicator" data-state="idle" aria-hidden="true" class="mail-pull-indicator">
                            <i class="fa-solid fa-rotate" data-mail--pull-to-refresh-target="icon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                            <span data-label-for="pulling">{{ 'pull_to_refresh.pull'|trans }}</span>
                            <span data-label-for="release">{{ 'pull_to_refresh.release'|trans }}</span>
                        </div>
                        {{ block('list_frame') }}
                    </div>

                    {% if not beside %}{{ readingElement }}{% endif %}
                </div>
            </section>
        {% endset %}
        {% set bodies = bodies|merge({ list: listBody }) %}

        {% if app.user %}
            {# ── The open message ────────────────────────────────────────
               The message's element when it sits beside the list, and what
               the pane says while nothing is open: a sibling of the message
               rather than content inside it, because mail--mail-pane REPLACES
               the message element's innerHTML with each message it loads, and
               a hint placed in it would be wiped by the first click. The hint
               carries the pane's menu, so an empty Message pane can still be
               split, moved or closed. #}
            {% set messageBody %}
                <section class="layout-pane flex flex-col min-w-0 min-h-0" {{ workspace.attrs(kinds.message) }} {{ maxKind == 'message' ? 'data-maxed' }} {{ firstTab == 'message' ? 'data-tab-current' }}>
                    <div class="main-pane layout-card flex flex-1 overflow-hidden relative"
                         {% if paneOpen and beside %}data-reading-open{% endif %}>
                        {% if beside %}{{ readingElement }}{% endif %}
                        <div data-reading-placeholder>
                            <div class="pane-header flex items-center justify-end px-2 border-b border-line">
                                {% include '_partials/_pane_controls.html.twig' only %}
                            </div>
                            <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col items-center justify-center gap-3" aria-hidden="true">
                                <i class="fa-regular fa-envelope-open" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                <p>{{ 'reading_pane.placeholder'|trans }}</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>
            {% endset %}

            {# ── The calendar ────────────────────────────────────────────
               Rendered with the page when it is on screen, so a reload shows
               mail and calendar together instead of a spinner that fills in a
               beat later; a lazy empty frame when it is parked, which
               layout--workspace points at `data-layout-src` the first time the
               pane is placed. Nobody pays for a calendar they are not looking
               at. #}
            {% set calendarBody %}
                <section class="layout-pane flex flex-col min-w-0 min-h-0" {{ workspace.attrs(kinds.calendar) }} {{ maxKind == 'calendar' ? 'data-maxed' }} {{ firstTab == 'calendar' ? 'data-tab-current' }}
                         data-layout-src="{{ path('app_calendar_pane') }}">
                    <div class="main-pane layout-card flex flex-col flex-1 overflow-hidden min-w-0">
                        {% if 'calendar' in present %}
                            {{ render(controller('App\\Controller\\Calendar\\CalendarController::pane')) }}
                        {% else %}
                            {{ _self.lazyFrame('calendar-pane-frame') }}
                        {% endif %}
                    </div>
                </section>
            {% endset %}

            {# ── The address book ────────────────────────────────────────
               The pane runs the whole address book: every contacts page renders
               into `contacts-pane-frame` when the frame asks for it (see
               contacts/_layout.html.twig), so a list, a contact, the editor and
               the merge all stay in the pane and the mail beside it never
               reloads. A block, because the full contacts page fills it with
               its own page (contacts/_page_shell.html.twig). #}
            {% set contactsBody %}
                <section class="layout-pane flex flex-col min-w-0 min-h-0" {{ workspace.attrs(kinds.contacts) }} {{ maxKind == 'contacts' ? 'data-maxed' }} {{ firstTab == 'contacts' ? 'data-tab-current' }}
                         data-layout-src="{{ path('app_contacts_list') }}">
                    <div class="main-pane layout-card flex flex-col flex-1 overflow-hidden min-w-0">
                        {% block contacts_pane_body %}
                            {% if 'contacts' in present %}
                                {{ render(controller('App\\Controller\\Contacts\\ContactController::list', { _contacts_pane: true })) }}
                            {% else %}
                                {{ _self.lazyFrame('contacts-pane-frame') }}
                            {% endif %}
                        {% endblock %}
                    </div>
                </section>
            {% endset %}

            {# ── The tasks ───────────────────────────────────────────────
               As the address book, and a block for its reason: /tasks fills it
               with its own page. #}
            {% set tasksBody %}
                <section class="layout-pane flex flex-col min-w-0 min-h-0" {{ workspace.attrs(kinds.tasks) }} {{ maxKind == 'tasks' ? 'data-maxed' }} {{ firstTab == 'tasks' ? 'data-tab-current' }}
                         data-layout-src="{{ path('app_tasks_index') }}"
                         data-controller="tasks--keep-scroll">
                    <div class="main-pane layout-card flex flex-col flex-1 overflow-hidden min-w-0">
                        {% block tasks_pane_body %}
                            {% if 'tasks' in present %}
                                {{ render(controller('App\\Controller\\Task\\TaskController::index', { _tasks_pane: true })) }}
                            {% else %}
                                {{ _self.lazyFrame('tasks-pane-frame') }}
                            {% endif %}
                        {% endblock %}
                    </div>
                </section>
            {% endset %}

            {# ── Further calendars ───────────────────────────────────────
               A workspace may show several calendars, each on its own view
               (LayoutPane, User::SETTING_CALENDAR_PANE_VIEWS). Each further one
               is drawn as the first is, in a frame of its own named by its id.
               One that is not on screen is not drawn at all: layout--workspace
               makes it from `calendarTemplate` below when a workspace places
               it. #}
            {% set furtherCalendars = {} %}
            {% for key in present|filter(key => key starts with 'calendar~') %}
                {% set paneId = key|split('~')|last %}
                {% set furtherBody %}
                    <section class="layout-pane flex flex-col min-w-0 min-h-0" {{ workspace.attrs(kinds.calendar, key) }} {{ maxKind == key ? 'data-maxed' }} {{ firstTab == key ? 'data-tab-current' }}
                             data-layout-src="{{ path('app_calendar_pane', { pane: paneId }) }}">
                        <div class="main-pane layout-card flex flex-col flex-1 overflow-hidden min-w-0">
                            {{ render(controller('App\\Controller\\Calendar\\CalendarController::pane', {}, { pane: paneId })) }}
                        </div>
                    </section>
                {% endset %}
                {% set furtherCalendars = furtherCalendars|merge({ (key): furtherBody }) %}
            {% endfor %}

            {% set bodies = bodies|merge({ message: messageBody, calendar: calendarBody, contacts: contactsBody, tasks: tasksBody })|merge(furtherCalendars) %}
        {% endif %}

        <div class="shell-frame flex h-full flex-col p-gutter gap-gutter"
             {% if app.user %}
             data-controller="layout--workspace"
             data-layout--workspace-document-value="{{ layout.document }}"
             data-layout--workspace-save-url-value="{{ path('app_layout_workspaces') }}"
             data-layout--workspace-token-value="{{ csrf_token('layout_workspaces') }}"
             data-layout--workspace-inbox-url-value="{{ path('app_mail_inbox') }}"
             data-layout--workspace-cookie-value="{{ constant('App\\Domain\\DTO\\Layout\\DeviceLayout::COOKIE') }}"
             {# A page that is one pane at full width: leaving full width there
                is a visit to the mail, because there is no list rendered
                behind it (see the contacts and tasks page shells). #}
             data-layout--workspace-page-value="{{ fullPane ?? '' }}"
             data-layout--workspace-strings-value="{{ _self.strings()|trim }}"
             data-layout--workspace-news-url-value="{{ path('app_layout_news') }}"
             data-layout--workspace-news-value="{{ layout.news|json_encode(constant('JSON_FORCE_OBJECT')) }}"
             data-layout--workspace-news-next-value="{{ layout.newsNext }}"
             data-layout--workspace-sidebar-url-value="{{ path('app_layout_sidebar') }}"
             data-layout--workspace-redraw-url-value="{{ path('app_layout_workspace_redraw', { id: '__ID__' }) }}"
             data-layout--workspace-groups-value="{{ layout.groupAccounts is empty ? '{}' : layout.groupAccounts|json_encode }}"
             {# The calendar's frame reports which view it landed on, and a
                week or a month needs more room than a day — see
                CalendarView::minimumPaneWidth(). turbo:frame-load bubbles, so
                the controller hears it from up here. #}
             data-action="turbo:frame-load->layout--workspace#frameLoaded keydown@window->layout--workspace#key resize@window->layout--workspace#resized"
             {% endif %}
        >

            {% include '_partials/_topbar.html.twig' with { workspaceShell: true } %}

            {% if app.user %}
                {% include '_layout/_workspace_edit.html.twig' %}
            {% endif %}

            {# Sidebar + workspace. data-workspace-row: where a workspace
               without a sidebar takes its left gutter back (#783, app.css). #}
            <div class="flex flex-1 gap-gutter overflow-hidden" data-workspace-row>

                {# Sidebar — desktop only (hidden on mobile, shown in drawer),
                   with its drag handle (#699). It takes its room from the
                   workspace, so the clamp stops before squeezing the panes
                   below a phone's width. #}
                {# The workspace's sidebar (#783): the Mail one, one of the
                   user's own, or none — a workspace of calendars and tasks
                   has no use for folders. Signed out there is no workspace,
                   and the Mail sidebar is what it always was. #}
                {# Always drawn, so a switch can bring a sidebar back without a
                   reload; hidden (data-no-sidebar) while there is none. #}
                {% include '_partials/_sidebar_pane.html.twig' with {
                    room:      '[data-layout-root]',
                    surface:   true,
                    template:  '_layout/_sidebar_frame.html.twig',
                    noSidebar: app.user and layout.sidebar is null,
                } %}

                <div class="layout-root"
                     data-layout-root
                     {% if app.user %}data-layout--workspace-target="root"{% endif %}
                     {% if maxKind %}data-layout-max="{{ maxKind }}"{% endif %}
                     {% if fullPane %}data-layout-page="{{ fullPane }}"{% endif %}
                     {% if beside %}data-message-beside{% endif %}>

                    {# Below lg the panes take turns: one at a time, chosen
                       here. The message is not a tab — it opens in place of
                       the list there, as it always did on a phone — so the
                       strip is drawn only when two other panes share the
                       workspace. Rebuilt by the controller when the layout
                       changes. #}
                    {% if app.user %}
                        <nav class="layout-tabs" data-layout--workspace-target="paneTabs"
                             aria-label="{{ 'layout.tabs.label'|trans }}"
                             {{ tabKinds|length < 2 ? 'hidden' }}>
                            {% for kind in tabKinds %}
                                <button type="button"
                                        class="layout-tab"
                                        data-action="layout--workspace#tab"
                                        data-layout--workspace-kind-param="{{ kind }}"
                                        aria-pressed="{{ kind == firstTab ? 'true' : 'false' }}">
                                    {# `kind` is a key: `calendar~<id>` for a further calendar. #}
                                    {% set shown = kinds[kind|split('~')|first] %}
                                    <i class="{{ shown.icon }}" aria-hidden="true"></i>{{ shown.transKey|trans }}
                                </button>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </nav>
                    {% endif %}

                    <div class="layout-body" {% if app.user %}data-layout--workspace-target="body"{% endif %}>
                        {% if app.user %}
                            {{ workspace.node(activeWorkspace.root, bodies) }}
                            {# A pane page whose pane the workspace does not show:
                               it is borrowed for the visit, at full width, and
                               goes back to the park when the page is left. #}
                            {% if fullPane and fullPane not in present %}
                                <div class="layout-cell" data-layout-borrowed>{{ bodies[fullPane] }}</div>
                            {% endif %}
                        {% else %}
                            {{ bodies.list }}
                        {% endif %}
                    </div>
                </div>

                {% if app.user %}
                    {# The panes this workspace does not show, rendered empty and
                       hidden until something places them. #}
                    <div class="layout-park" hidden data-layout--workspace-target="park">
                        {% for kind in enum('App\\Domain\\Enum\\Layout\\PaneKind').cases() %}
                            {% if kind.value not in present and kind.value != fullPane %}
                                {{ bodies[kind.value] }}
                            {% endif %}
                        {% endfor %}
                    </div>

                    {# A further calendar, for layout--workspace to make when a
                       workspace places one this page did not draw: __KEY__ and
                       __ID__ are its key and id. Empty and lazy, like a parked
                       pane. #}
                    <template data-layout--workspace-target="calendarTemplate">
                        <section class="layout-pane flex flex-col min-w-0 min-h-0" {{ workspace.attrs(kinds.calendar, '__KEY__') }}
                                 data-layout-src="{{ path('app_calendar_pane') }}?pane=__ID__">
                            <div class="main-pane layout-card flex flex-col flex-1 overflow-hidden min-w-0">
                                {{ _self.lazyFrame('calendar-pane-frame-__ID__') }}
                            </div>
                        </section>
                    </template>
                {% endif %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# One snooze menu and one spam menu for every row of the list. #}
    {% include '_partials/_row_menus.html.twig' %}
{% endblock %}

{#
The list itself, in one place.

Its own block because it is rendered two ways: inside the page above on a
visit, and alone as the whole response when the poll refreshes it. The page
templates fill `message_list`, `inbox_tabs` and the toolbar without caring
which of the two is happening.
#}
{% block list_frame %}
    {# Whether this page actually put a list in the frame. The thread page does
       not — it has no list to render — and the pane controller reads this to
       know that going Back has to fetch one rather than uncover an empty
       frame. See mail_pane_controller#_listNeedsRendering. #}
    {# `data-sync-scope` is ON THE FRAME, which is a change of address rather
       than of meaning: it used to sit on #message-list, one element further
       out. That element is outside the frame, so it survives a frame
       navigation — and a scope that survives the navigation is a scope that
       describes the folder you just left. Switch from the inbox to Sent and
       the list went on refreshing itself for inbox syncs and ignoring sent
       ones, which shows up as new mail not appearing until something else
       redraws. The scope belongs where the list it describes is rendered.
       Read by mail--mail-pane#_affectsCurrentView and by compose--compose
       when it discards a draft. #}
    <turbo-frame id="inbox-list-frame" data-turbo-action="advance" class="contents"
                 data-list-rendered="{% block list_rendered %}1{% endblock %}"
                 data-sync-scope="{% block list_scope %}*{% endblock %}"
                 data-list-scope-value="{{ block('list_scope_value') }}"
                 data-unified-inbox="{% block unified_inbox_id %}{% endblock %}">

        {# ── Gmail-style toolbar ──────────────────────────────────────────── #}
        {% block inbox_toolbar %}
            {# list_scope is the same block the sync scope uses, so a view
               names itself once. list_scope_value carries the one thing that
               varies within a scope: the category tab for the inbox, the label
               id for a label view. Both go to the server's closed set — see
               App\Service\Mail\ListViewResolver — through list_view_scope and
               list_view_value, which only a search overrides. #}
            {% include '_partials/_list_toolbar.html.twig' with {
                page:             page     ?? 1,
                total:            total    ?? 0,
                per_page:         per_page ?? 50,
                list_scope:       block('list_view_scope'),
                list_scope_value: block('list_view_value'),
                unread_only:      unread_only ?? false,
            } %}
        {% endblock %}

        {# Optional tabs row (inbox only).

           The wrapper is always here even when the row is not, because it is
           what a refresh writes into: `data-list-region` marks the parts of
           this frame a background refresh is allowed to replace, and a region
           that disappears when it is empty cannot be filled again. Only the
           inbox renders tabs, and only when more than one category has mail —
           see mail/inbox.html.twig — so the empty case is the common one.

           `contents`, so the wrapper adds no box of its own and the tabs stay
           a direct child of the frame's flex column. #}
        <div data-list-region="tabs" class="contents">{% block inbox_tabs %}{% endblock %}</div>

        {# The rows are a region too. A refresh brings them up to date by
           morphing rather than replacing — see mail--mail-pane#_morphRows.

           `data-enter-children`, not `data-enter`: the whole-list gesture is
           the ROWS arriving, not this element. A list that fades as one block
           tells you a grey rectangle changed; fifty rows dropping in, each one
           a frame behind the one above it, tells you what changed. motion.js
           gives each row the entrance named here and marks it as spoken for, so
           the entrance the row carries for itself — the long drop that means
           one new mail, see _partials/_thread_row.html.twig — cannot fire on
           top. Same direction as that one, and nothing else in common: this is
           30ms a row, and what is seen is the cascade rather than the fall.

           This element is only ever FILLED by a refresh, never replaced, so it
           fires on exactly the thing it should mean: a genuinely different list
           arriving, from the sidebar, a category tab, the next page or a search.

           The stagger is capped in CSS at eight rows and that cap is doing real
           work here. Fifty rows at 16ms would be four fifths of a second before
           the last one was legible; capped, a list of fifty and a list of six
           both finish in about 150ms and read as one cascade. #}
        <ul data-list-region="rows"
            data-enter-children="slide-down"
            data-enter-stagger
            class="flex-1 overflow-y-auto divide-y divide-line">
            {% block message_list %}{% endblock %}
        </ul>

        {# The long-press action sheet, once per list. A <template>, so it is inert until a hold
           copies it, and it sits after the rows region, outside every data-list-region: a refresh
           morphs the rows and the tabs and never this. See _partials/_action_sheet.html.twig. #}
        {% include '_partials/_action_sheet.html.twig' %}

    </turbo-frame>
{% endblock %}

{# A pane's frame before anything has asked for it: empty, lazy and without a
   src, which layout--workspace sets from the pane's data-layout-src the first
   time it places the pane. #}
{% macro lazyFrame(id) %}
    <turbo-frame id="{{ id }}" loading="lazy" data-layout-lazy class="flex flex-col min-h-0 flex-1">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-24 text-ink-faint" aria-live="polite">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-circle-notch fa-spin" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        </div>
    </turbo-frame>
{% endmacro %}

{# What the controller says and labels, translated here so no English lives in
   the script. One JSON object, read once on connect. #}
{% macro strings() %}
    {{- {
        kinds:        enum('App\\Domain\\Enum\\Layout\\PaneKind').cases()|reduce((carry, kind) => carry|merge({ (kind.value): kind.transKey|trans }), {}),
        noRoom:       'layout.toast.no_room'|trans,
        onlyPane:     'layout.toast.only_pane'|trans,
        atEdge:       'layout.toast.at_edge'|trans,
        saveFailed:   'layout.toast.save_failed'|trans,
        saved:        'layout.toast.saved'|trans,
        deleted:      'layout.toast.deleted'|trans,
        newName:      'layout.workspace.new_name'|trans,
        editFirst:    'layout.toast.edit_first'|trans,
        noList:       'layout.toast.no_list'|trans,
        allShown:     'layout.toast.all_shown'|trans,
        widePercent:  'layout.edit.percent_wide'|trans,
        highPercent:  'layout.edit.percent_high'|trans,
        applied:      'layout.toast.applied'|trans,
        deleteConfirm: 'layout.edit.delete_confirm'|trans,
        divider:      'layout.divider'|trans,
        dividerHint:  'layout.divider_hint'|trans,
        dropSwap:     'layout.drop.swap'|trans,
        dropReplace:  'layout.drop.replace'|trans,
        drop_left:    'layout.drop.left'|trans,
        drop_right:   'layout.drop.right'|trans,
        drop_top:     'layout.drop.top'|trans,
        drop_bottom:  'layout.drop.bottom'|trans,
        newCount:     'layout.workspace.new'|trans({ '%count%': '%count%' }),
        soon:         'layout.workspace.soon'|trans,
        moreWorkspaces: 'layout.workspace.more'|trans,
    }|json_encode -}}
{% endmacro %}
